[38;2;124;124;124mWe have a 20×20 grid of sites (positive integers from 1 to 20, so 400 sites). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mAmy places red stones, Ben places blue stones, Amy goes first. Amy's constraint:[0m[38;2;124;124;124many two red stones must not be at distance √5. Ben has no constraint. They stop [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhen a player can't place.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need the greatest K such that Amy can guarantee placing at least K red [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstones.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mDistance √5 means: two sites (x1,y1) and (x2,y2) with (x1-x2)² + (y1-y2)² = 5. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo the differences are (±1, ±2) or (±2, ±1). This is the knight's move in chess.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo Amy needs to place red stones such that no two red stones are a knight's move[0m[38;2;124;124;124mapart. This is like an independent set in the "knight's move graph" on the 20×20[0m[38;2;124;124;124mgrid.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut there's the adversarial element: Ben is also placing stones (blue), which [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moccupy sites that Amy can't use.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. The game: Amy and Ben alternate, Amy first. Amy places [0m[38;2;124;124;124mred on unoccupied site with no red at distance √5. Ben places blue on any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munoccupied site. Stop when someone can't move.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAmy wants to maximize her red stones. Ben wants to minimize Amy's red stones [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(presumably by occupying sites and blocking Amy).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, Ben's goal is to minimize the number of red stones Amy places. Ben can[0m[38;2;124;124;124mplace blue stones anywhere to block Amy.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe game ends when a player can't place. If Amy can't place, she stops. If Ben [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcan't place (all sites occupied), Ben stops. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTotal sites = 400. If the game goes to completion with all sites filled, Amy [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mplaces 200 and Ben places 200 (Amy first, so Amy gets ceil(400/2) = 200). But [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mAmy might be blocked earlier.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the game stops when a player cannot place. So if Amy can't place a red[0m[38;2;124;124;124mstone (all remaining unoccupied sites are at distance √5 from some red stone, or[0m[38;2;124;124;124mno unoccupied sites), Amy stops. If Ben can't place (no unoccupied sites), Ben [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstops.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAmy wants to guarantee at least K red stones regardless of Ben's play.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the structure. The key insight is that Amy needs an [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mindependent set in the knight's graph. Ben is adversarially removing vertices.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about what the knight's graph looks like on a 20×20 board. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey observation: The knight's graph is bipartite. A knight move changes the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcolor of the square (in checkerboard coloring) — actually, a knight move from [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(x,y) to (x±1, y±2) or (x±2, y±1). The parity of x+y changes by 1+2=3 (odd) or [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2+1=3 (odd). So yes, the knight's graph is bipartite with the checkerboard [0m
